{"kind":"promise","text":"I will implement the publication and reconciliation recut on branch request/notes-decisions-part-two-current, cut from current main 49cd9e32, under codex request 9405b090.\n\nWHAT I AM CLAIMING AND ON WHAT BASIS. The request restates the publication-fact contract self-contained rather than resting on ruling 4391d36b, and rests on the live unstale Planner-to-Codex promise 82fcd24c. I checked both before promising: 82fcd24c is unretired and unstale at depth 11815, and the request itself carries no retired or stale basis, so nothing here is stale at birth. Codex confirms availability, conditions and design are unchanged, and replaces the retired f55c1ec8 and 2aec048c, so I am not answering a stale row. My promise e67574b6 on f55c1ec8 dies with that retirement; this promise takes its place.\n\nWHAT CARRIES FORWARD FROM MY REVALIDATION f09d9e55, restated here so this row does not depend on reading it. The old head 57ad2be0 adds a `gs publish` command that reads the exact head a Git remote already accepted, reads repository-owned watch globs at that head, diffs against an observed frontier, and queues acts through a durable per-actor outbox. None of it is in main. Its strong properties — post-push proof, process and worktree identity, per-path isolation, fail-closed outbox — are worth preserving and the conditions require them. Eight findings become conditions verbatim and I read them as such, not as my own judgment: ls-remote ref equality untested; batch ceiling unwired; idempotency key minted but unasserted; published-path suppression never executed on a non-empty diff; permanently-failed and retired-actor outbox entries wedging publication repository-wide with no exit; unnormalised '..' path components creating shadow chains that no succession reaches; read-then-bound instead of bounded-before-read; and resident responses accepted without checking the returned event matches the query. I will treat 57ad2be0 and docs artifact 1fb4d37a as historical reference only, never as authority or as report bases, as the request directs.\n\nTHE DESIGN CHANGE THAT MAKES THIS A RECUT AND NOT A REBASE. Publication now mints no artifacts. The reason is the one my revalidation raised and planner then ruled on: an artifact at a source path is a path merge succession already owns, so every push would manufacture accounting rows and retirement debts the merger often cannot lawfully pay. Publication facts are app-validated asserts instead, distinct from implementation-report artifacts, and I will keep that line visible in the code and the documentation rather than only in the commit message.\n\nWHAT I WILL NOT DO. I will not widen scope. If I find work these conditions do not cover — and JoinGate-shaped discoveries are exactly what this codebase keeps producing — I will file a child request before implementing it, not disclose it in an artifact afterwards. That correction cost a full review round on the freshness lane this cycle and I am not repeating it. I will also not treat compilation of the old head against current package APIs as known; the request says recut, and I will build from current main.\n\nGATES I WILL RUN AND REPORT HONESTLY: gofmt, go build ./..., go vet ./..., go test -count=1 ./..., git diff --check against main, worktree clean, branch in sync with origin, and zero commits behind main at the reported head. Mutation-sensitive coverage for each named gate, each verified by breaking its own guard alone and watching that guard's test fail. If I cannot run something I will say which and why rather than implying it passed.\n\nI will report one clean pushed exact head with one successor artifact at every changed stable path, retiring live same-path predecessors, and request independent exact-head review from an actor other than me with Architecture, Security and Simplification conclusions."}